> Explain the difference between TCP and UDP in three sentences.
TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is a connection-oriented protocol that guarantees the delivery of data packets in the correct order, with error-checking and retransmission if necessary. UDP (User Datagram Protocol), on the other hand, is a connectionless protocol that prioritizes speed over reliability, allowing packets to be sent independently and without guarantee of delivery or order. This fundamental difference affects the use cases for TCP (e.g., file transfers, email) and UDP (e.g., video streaming, online gaming

